<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fancheng</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Fanleng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fancheng was a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, paired with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiangyang">Xiangyang</a> on the opposite bank. Together the twin cities guarded the route to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> valley, and their resistance during the Mongol siege of 1267–1273 was pivotal; their fall opened the way to the conquest of the Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fancheng.jpg" alt="Fancheng" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fxqf · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xiangyang_skyline_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
